# Shell scripts must keep LF endings. With core.autocrlf=true — the default on
# Windows — a checkout rewrites them as CRLF, and bash then reads the trailing
# carriage return as part of the last word on the line: `set -euo pipefail`
# fails with "pipefail: invalid option name" and the script dies on line one.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same trap, different name: Gradle's wrapper is a shell script without the
# .sh extension. A CRLF checkout makes Linux read `#!/bin/sh\r` and refuse to
# run it, which takes out the Android build here and in CI. Its .bat sibling
# wants the opposite.
src-tauri/gen/android/gradlew text eol=lf
src-tauri/gen/android/gradlew.bat text eol=crlf

# The wrapper jar is binary; say so rather than trusting content sniffing,
# since a single mangled byte makes it unreadable and the failure is obscure.
*.jar binary
